1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,064
Put one person on a rope

2
00:00:02,064 --> 00:00:04,729
and they pull at full strength.

3
00:00:04,729 --> 00:00:07,222
Put eight people on that rope

4
00:00:07,222 --> 00:00:08,770
and half vanishes.

5
00:00:08,770 --> 00:00:12,150
French agricultural engineer Maximilien Ringelmann published

6
00:00:12,150 --> 00:00:13,615
the exact numbers in 1913:

7
00:00:13,615 --> 00:00:15,981
solo pullers delivered one hundred percent

8
00:00:15,981 --> 00:00:16,995
of their capacity,

9
00:00:16,995 --> 00:00:19,136
pairs dropped to ninety-three percent,

10
00:00:19,136 --> 00:00:21,840
and groups of eight averaged forty-nine percent.

11
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:24,756
Modern offices recreate Ringelmann's rope every morning:

12
00:00:24,756 --> 00:00:27,463
eight-person status calls blur individual ownership,

13
00:00:27,463 --> 00:00:29,077
multiply coordination friction,

14
00:00:29,077 --> 00:00:31,420
and make coasting invisible inside the crowd.

15
00:00:31,420 --> 00:00:33,482
Protect single-threaded ownership:

16
00:00:33,482 --> 00:00:35,967
assign one clear builder per deliverable,

17
00:00:35,967 --> 00:00:37,665
keep working units to one or

18
00:00:37,665 --> 00:00:38,332
two people,

19
00:00:38,332 --> 00:00:41,000
and automate routine handoffs with software.

20
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:43,120
Shrink the rope until every name

21
00:00:43,120 --> 00:00:44,842
owns a measurable outcome,

22
00:00:44,842 --> 00:00:47,690
and tiny teams outship bloated departments.
